            <ArticleTitle>Orchestration of epithelial-derived cytokines and innate immune cells in allergic airway inflammation.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1359-6101(17)30167-3</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cytogfr.2017.11.004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Allergic asthma, a chronic respiratory disease, is a leading worldwide health problem, which inflames and constricts the airways, leading to breathing difficulty. Many studies have focused on the pathogenesis contributed by the adaptive immune system, including CD4(+) T lymphocytes in delayed type hypersensitivity and B cell-produced IgE in anaphylaxis. More recently, a focus on the airway mucosal barrier and the innate immune system has highlighted, in coordination with T and B cells, to initiate and establish disease. This review highlights the impacts of epithelial-derived cytokines and innate immune cells on allergic airway reactions.</AbstractText>
